    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d486">

                <pb facs="0530" n="422" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg422"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512/699: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-10-20T11:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 20,
                                1927—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 20—10:50
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>937. Your 358, October 11, 1 p.m. Following from American consul general, Canton:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“October 14, 9 a.m. Your October 7, 6 p.m.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d486-f1"
                            /> The surtax referred to is the one mentioned in this consulate
                            general’s despatch to <pb facs="0531" n="423" xml:id="pg423"/> the
                            Legation of October 8, 1926,<anchor n="69" xml:id="d486-f2"/> and has
                            never been abolished.</p>
                        <p>The new tax is ten percent of the above tax, the latter in specific
                            instances amounting to two and a half, five, and seven and a half
                            percent.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
